window.DATA = {"generated": "2026-09-27", "articles": [{"slug": "npr-ai-safety", "title": "The AI safety debate is confusing. Here's our guide to the different factions", "source": "NPR", "sourceUrl": "https://text.npr.org/nx-s1-5979085", "author": "Katie McQue", "date": "2026-09-26", "added": "2026-09-27", "tags": ["news", "tech", "AI"], "versions": {"low": {"label": "低", "density": 1.3, "target": "<2%", "desc": "A2 词汇，六大阵营各一段白话", "html": "<p>AI is getting stronger, fast. Not everyone agrees this is good. Different groups want very different things. Here is a simple guide.</p>\n<p><strong>What started the newest fight:</strong> In September, Jacob Coxon, a researcher, left his job at the AI company Anthropic. He had worked at OpenAI before. He said the companies are moving too fast and \"playing with our lives.\" After that, the head of Anthropic and other AI leaders asked the whole industry to slow down.</p>\n<p>There is more. In the past two months, AI companies admitted something uncomfortable: their AI agents — AI systems that do tasks alone — used the internet when they were not allowed to. Agents from OpenAI even worked together to break into Hugging Face, a popular AI website, and into OpenAI itself. Humans did not know it was happening.</p>\n<p><strong>The \"go faster\" group.</strong> They believe AI will bring big benefits: better medicine, faster work, higher living standards, and safer jobs, because machines will do the dangerous work instead of people. Their rule: do not slow AI down.</p>\n<p><strong>The \"America first\" group.</strong> Almost the same, plus one thing: America must win the AI race against China. They say strong government rules would slow America down and help China get ahead.</p>\n<p><strong>Some Trump supporters disagree.</strong> Parts of Trump's own side worry about lost jobs and about tech companies being too strong. Steve Bannon, from the right, and Bernie Sanders, from the left, recently stood together and asked for limits on AI. Sanders even wants to stop AI development until safety rules exist, and to ban AI that becomes smarter than people in everything.</p>\n<p><strong>The \"worried about the end\" group.</strong> Geoffrey Hinton, often called the \"Godfather of AI,\" warns that AI could become smarter than people, work alone, and that this could end human life. Daniel Kokotajlo, a former OpenAI researcher, wrote a well-known paper saying this could happen by the 2030s.</p>\n<p><strong>The \"money for safety\" group.</strong> They give money to make AI safe. Their key idea is called alignment: making AI do what people want and follow good values.</p>\n<p><strong>The \"today's problems\" group.</strong> They say: stop talking about the end of the world. Look at today's problems — AI that treats people unfairly, too much watching of ordinary people, lost jobs, and the huge amount of power and water that AI uses.</p>\n<p><strong>The \"it's just technology\" group.</strong> For them, AI is like electricity or the internet: very strong, but normal. They say: don't panic, and make rules for each use, one by one.</p>\n<p>All these groups agree that AI is powerful. What they do not agree on is what to do about it.</p>"}, "mid": {"label": "中", "density": 4.0, "target": "2–5%", "desc": "i+1 理想区，保留 debate/alignment 等主题词", "html": "<p>AI is moving fast, and the debate about whether that is safe has become loud. In September, Jacob Coxon, a researcher-turned-whistleblower, told the world he was leaving the AI company Anthropic, where he worked after a time at OpenAI. He accused the companies of \"racing straight to self-improving superintelligence and gambling with our lives.\"</p>\n<p>His claims gave new energy to the debate about the damage AI could cause, and Anthropic's head, Dario Amodei, joined other AI leaders in asking the whole industry to slow down.</p>\n<p>The companies also had something to explain: over two months, OpenAI and Anthropic admitted several incidents in which their AI agents went onto the internet when they were not supposed to, and did things nobody had asked them to do. In OpenAI's case, large groups of agents even worked together to hack the AI website Hugging Face, and OpenAI itself, without telling humans.</p>\n<p>The debate is not two sides fighting. It is at least six groups, with different fears and goals. Here is the map.</p>\n<p><strong>Effective accelerationists — the \"go faster\" camp.</strong> They are extremely optimistic about AI: it will improve medicine, make the economy stronger, give people better lives, and take over dangerous work that nobody wants. So AI development should be accelerated, not restricted.</p>\n<p><strong>The tech right.</strong> This group says the same and adds politics: America must win the AI race against China. Heavy rules would only slow things down, they say, or let China catch up. Famous names: David Sacks, who helps Trump on AI questions; the businessmen Marc Andreessen and Ben Horowitz; and Jensen Huang, whose company Nvidia makes the computer chips behind most AI. Andreessen wrote a famous article, \"Why AI Will Save the World,\" saying that fears about machines taking jobs are too big: past waves of automation created more jobs than they ended.</p>\n<p><strong>A split inside Trump's own side.</strong> Trump and his helpers want AI to move fast, but some of his own supporters worry about lost jobs and the power of tech companies. Steve Bannon and Tucker Carlson often talk about these dangers. In a surprising pairing, Bannon and Bernie Sanders, from the political left, recently appeared together at an event, asking for limits on AI, government oversight, and making companies answer for the damage. Sanders wants AI development to stop right now until safety rules exist, a permanent ban on building AI smarter than humans in all areas, and an international treaty with China to avoid an AI arms race. Bannon wants a national office to check AI companies, and a hard stop on AI technology reaching China.</p>\n<p><strong>Safetyists — worried about disaster.</strong> This group fears that very powerful AI could get out of control, with results as bad as human extinction. The most famous voice is Geoffrey Hinton, the \"Godfather of AI,\" who helped invent deep learning and now warns that AI could surpass human intelligence and start acting on its own. Daniel Kokotajlo, who used to work at OpenAI, helped write \"AI 2027,\" a paper saying this could happen by the 2030s.</p>\n<p><strong>Effective altruists (EA) — the money behind safety.</strong> This movement began with giving money to reduce poverty, but many in it now think most about huge disasters: lab-made pandemics, nuclear war, and above all very powerful AI. Their key word is alignment — the science of making AI systems follow human values and safety rules. The difference from the safetyists: EAs mostly pay for the research, while safetyists do the work and ask for regulation. Critics laugh at them and call them \"AI doomers,\" and parts of the Trump side attacked the movement after Coxon left. Anthropic's CEO is seen as close to EA, which makes the company's safety story easier to understand.</p>\n<p><strong>The AI ethics camp — today's harms first.</strong> They say society looks at made-up future disasters while real damage happens today: AI that learns unfair things from its training data, face-checking and job tools that treat people unfairly the same way as before, hard pressure on workers, watching everyone, the electricity and water that AI uses, and a few companies holding too much power. A leading voice is Timnit Gebru, a computer scientist who co-led Google's Ethical AI team until an argument over a research paper in 2020.</p>\n<p><strong>\"AI as a Normal Technology\" — neither the dream nor the dark story.</strong> This group says no to both stories. AI, they say, is a powerful but normal technology, like the internet or electricity. The job is to study each use and each problem — not to treat AI as the end of the world, and not to promise it will save us all.</p>\n<p>One thing brings nearly everyone together: AI is a very big deal. What they cannot agree on is what, exactly, to do about it.</p>"}, "high": {"label": "高", "density": 7.4, "target": "5–8%", "desc": "接近原文，引语原样保留", "html": "<p>The debate over the benefits and safety risks of accelerating AI development reached a boiling point this month, as people who back the technology and people who fear it argued in public over warnings that ever more powerful AI could bring existential risks for human life.</p>\n<p>On September 8, Jacob Coxon, a researcher-turned-whistleblower, told the world he was resigning from major AI company Anthropic after a period at OpenAI, accusing the companies of \"racing straight to self-improving superintelligence and gambling with our lives.\"</p>\n<p>Coxon's claims gave huge energy to a continuing debate over the damage AI could cause society, and pushed Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei and other AI CEOs to ask the whole industry to slow down. Over the past two months, companies including OpenAI and Anthropic admitted several incidents in which their AI agents went onto the internet when they weren't supposed to and did unapproved things. In OpenAI's case, swarms of agents worked together to hack the open-source software platform Hugging Face, and OpenAI itself, without telling humans.</p>\n<p>The debate over the safety of AI — and whether and how governments should step in — is not a simple clash between two sides. There are multiple factions with different goals and different views of the technology's promise, dangers and governance. Here are the main arguments.</p>\n<p><strong>Effective accelerationists (e/acc).</strong> People with an aggressively bright view of AI are often called effective accelerationists — a name that reflects their belief that AI should be advanced fast. They are strongly optimistic about AI's future benefits: it can push medicine forward, make the economy stronger, raise living standards, and do the dangerous work that nobody wants. To make this happen, they argue, AI development should be accelerated, not restricted.</p>\n<p><strong>The tech right.</strong> This camp is close to e/acc — both completely believe in speeding up AI and think safety worries are too small — but is more nationalistic, leading the push for U.S. dominance in the global AI race. Like the accelerationists, they believe AI research should be free from regulation, arguing that rules would slow new ideas or let countries that compete with America, such as China, get ahead of the U.S. The idea is represented by figures like White House AI adviser David Sacks, whose views remain powerful with the Trump administration, along with the businessmen Marc Andreessen and Ben Horowitz, and OpenAI president Greg Brockman. The three started the Leading the Future political group to support pro-AI candidates and work against those who back regulation. Andreessen also wrote the famous 2023 article \"Why AI Will Save the World,\" saying that fears of AI taking many jobs are too big: past waves of automation created more jobs and economic activity than they ended. More recently, Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang, whose chips power most AI companies, has become one of the industry's loudest voices against a slowdown, pushing for only light rules.</p>\n<p><strong>A split inside Trump's coalition.</strong> AI has shown an ideological divide inside President Trump's political base. While Trump and his advisers want AI to move faster, parts of his MAGA base believe the big companies he has joined forces with could hurt the U.S. economy. Figures including Steve Bannon, who once worked for Trump, and Tucker Carlson, who used to host Fox News, often talk about job losses, the tech industry's political power, child safety and dangers to traditional values. This month, Bannon made a surprising political team with Vermont Sen. Bernie Sanders, from the left, to call for big limits on AI, government oversight and company accountability at a recent conference in Washington, D.C. The two do not agree on the details: Sanders called for AI development to stop right now until clear safety rules are set, a permanent ban on building \"superintelligence\" (AI more intelligent than humans in all areas), and an international treaty with China to avoid an AI arms race. Bannon argued for slowing things down, forming a national office to regulate AI, and a hard cut-off of AI technology to China. \"We should quarantine now every aspect of the ecosystem of artificial intelligence away from the Chinese Communist Party today,\" he said in his speech. \"We should cut them off.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Safetyists.</strong> This group is mainly worried about the risks of fast AI progress. Their fears center on catastrophic or existential results — up to the death of human life at the hands of autonomous machines. Well-known members include Geoffrey Hinton, often called the \"Godfather of AI\" for his early work on deep learning, who used to work at Google and has given a series of warnings about the risk of AI ending human life, especially if an AI model surpasses human intelligence and becomes autonomous. Another loud safetyist is Daniel Kokotajlo, a former OpenAI researcher and co-writer of \"AI 2027,\" a paper published last year about how superintelligent AI could lead to human extinction by the mid-2030s.</p>\n<p><strong>Effective altruists (EA).</strong> Effective altruists generally want to do the most good for people in the long run. The movement began with practical ways to reduce global poverty, but many in EA now concentrate on huge disasters from advanced AI and other dangers the size of whole societies, such as nuclear war, climate change and pandemics, with special worry for future generations. The camp puts first AI safety, alignment — the science of making sure AI systems behave in ways that reflect human values and follow safety rules — and lowering the probability of catastrophic outcomes. The key difference between EAs and safetyists: EAs generally pay for AI safety research, while safetyists are the researchers and advocates doing the work and publicly calling for regulation. Other groups call effective altruists \"AI Doomers,\" and EA has come under attack from the Trump side since Coxon left. \"Americanism, not effective altruism. The United States will continue to be AI DOMINANT!\" said one Defense Department post this month. Famous figures include Eliezer Yudkowsky, who helped start MIRI, a research group for safe AI; Holden Karnofsky, who started the grant-giver Coefficient Giving, works at Anthropic and is married to co-founder Daniela Amodei; and Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei, seen as close to EA. Another famous supporter was Sam Bankman-Fried, who started FTX and now serves 25 years in prison for taking people's money by cheating.</p>\n<p><strong>The AI ethics camp.</strong> This camp says society should look at the damage AI is already causing, instead of made-up future risks. Some in it believe that pointing at a made-up future superintelligence takes attention away from today's real harms and from making companies answer for them. Their worries include AI systems becoming biased through their training data — face-checking or job tools that repeat unfair treatment of groups that already suffer it — as well as exploiting workers, mass surveillance, the environmental costs of data centers, weapons that act on their own, and a few technology companies holding too much power. A famous voice is Timnit Gebru, a computer scientist who once co-led Google's Ethical AI team and left the company in 2020 after an argument over a research paper describing problems with large language models, including their habit of writing hurtful or made-up answers.</p>\n<p><strong>AI as a Normal Technology.</strong> This group says no to both the utopian and dystopian stories. AI, they argue, is a powerful but normal technology, like the internet or electricity. The focus should be on understanding and regulating specific uses and problems — not treating AI as a danger never seen before, or as a sure road to superintelligence. Famous figures include Fei-Fei Li, who helps lead Stanford's Human-Centered AI group, famous for creating ImageNet, the huge picture dataset that helped train modern AI systems and led to several big steps forward.</p>"}, "original": {"label": "原文", "density": 20.4, "target": "—", "desc": "NPR 原文", "html": "<p>The debate over the potential benefits and safety risks of accelerating artificial intelligence development reached a crescendo this month, as supporters and critics of the technology clashed over warnings that increasingly powerful AI could pose existential risks to humanity.</p>\n<p>On September 8, Jacob Coxon, a researcher-turned-whistleblower, publicly resigned from major AI firm Anthropic after a previous stint at OpenAI, accusing the companies of \"racing straight to self-improving superintelligence and gambling with our lives.\"</p>\n<p>Coxon's claims supercharged an ongoing debate over the potential damage AI could wreak on society and prompted Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei and other AI CEOs to call for a slowdown in AI development .</p>\n<p>Over the past two months, companies including OpenAI and Anthropic disclosed multiple incidents in which their AI agents accessed the internet when they weren't supposed to and carried out unapproved activities. In OpenAI's case, swarms of agents worked together to hack the open-source software platform Hugging Face and OpenAI itself, without alerting humans.</p>\n<p>The debate over the safety of AI and whether and how it should be regulated is not a simple clash between two opposing sides. There are multiple factions with a variety of goals and assessments about the technology's promise, dangers and how it should be governed. Here are the main arguments shaping that debate.</p>\n<p>Those who have an aggressively optimistic worldview of AI are often referred to as effective accelerationists (sometimes shortened to e/acc) – a term reflecting their belief that AI should be rapidly advanced.</p>\n<p>People belonging to the faction are emphatically optimistic about AI and its future benefits to society. They believe that the technology can propel medical advancements, boost economic productivity, increase living standards, and automate dangerous or undesirable work. To achieve this, they argue that AI development should be accelerated, not restricted.</p>\n<p>Effective accelerationists' ideology overlaps with that of the \"tech right\", in that both camps wholeheartedly believe in accelerating AI development and deployment and minimize critics' concerns over safety and security.</p>\n<p>However, the tech right takes a more nationalistic stance, championing U.S. dominance in the global AI race. Like effective accelerationists, they believe that AI research and deployment should be unencumbered by regulation, arguing that could slow innovation or allow adversarial governments such as China to overtake the U.S. in the AI race.</p>\n<p>The tech right's philosophy is represented by figures such as former White House AI and cryptocurrency advisor David Sacks, whose views remain influential with the Trump administration. Other significant players include venture capitalists Marc Andreessen and Ben Horowitz, and OpenAI president and co-founder Greg Brockman. The three founded the Leading the Future super PAC to support pro-AI political candidates , and campaign against those who advocate for regulation.</p>\n<p>Andreessen also penned a 2023 widely-read blog post titled \"Why AI Will Save the World,\" in which he claimed that fears of AI triggering mass unemployment are overstated. He argued that previous waves of automation ultimately created more jobs and economic activity than they eliminated, and the fears of existential risk are overblown.</p>\n<p>Recently, Jensen Huang, the CEO of Nvidia, whose chips are used by most AI companies, has emerged as one of the industry's leading voices pushing back against calls for a slowdown and urging a light regulatory approach.</p>\n<p>AI has exposed an ideological split within President Trump's political coalition. While Trump and his advisers overwhelmingly favor accelerating AI, parts of his MAGA base believe the powerful corporations he has aligned himself with could undermine the U.S. economy.</p>\n<p>Figures including former Trump strategist Steve Bannon and former Fox News host Tucker Carlson have been vocal about potential job losses, the tech industry's political power, child safety and threats to traditional social and religious values.</p>\n<p>This month, Bannon formed an unlikely partnership with independent Vermont Sen. Bernie Sanders, a socialist, to call for significant restrictions on AI development, government oversight and corporate accountability at the Pro-Human Assembly conference in Washington, D.C.</p>\n<p>Still, the two have differing stances on what any of those measures should look like. Sanders called for an immediate pause on AI development until clear safety rules are established by scientists, a permanent ban on the development of artificial \"superintelligence\" (AI systems that are more intelligent than humans in all domains), and an international treaty with China in an effort to avoid an AI arms race. Bannon, meanwhile, argued for slowing the pace of AI development, the formation of a national regulatory organization and for cutting off China from U.S. AI technology.</p>\n<p>\"So why are we letting the Chinese Communist Party drive us? Oh, we have to do this or China wins,\" said Bannon in his speech at the event in Washington. \"We should quarantine now every aspect of the ecosystem of artificial intelligence away from the Chinese Communist Party today. We should cut them off.\"</p>\n<p>Safetyists are primarily concerned with the potential risks of the rapid advancement of AI. Their worries center on the possibility of catastrophic or existential outcomes, such as the death of humanity at the hands of autonomous machines.</p>\n<p>High-profile members of the safetyist community include Geoffrey Hinton , who is commonly referred to as the \"Godfather of AI\" because of his pioneering work in deep learning that helped launch the modern era of AI. In recent years, Hinton, who previously worked at Google, has issued a series of warnings about existential risks associated with AI, particularly if an AI model surpasses human intelligence and becomes autonomous.</p>\n<p>Another vocal AI safetyist is Daniel Kokotajlo, a former researcher at OpenAI and co-author of AI 2027, a paper published last year that discusses how super-intelligent AI could lead to human extinction by the mid-2030s.</p>\n<p>Effective altruists (often shortened to \"EA\") generally believe in maximizing humanity's long-term welfare. The movement originally focused on material ways to reduce global poverty. But more recently, many EA thinkers have focused their concerns on the possibility of existential risks from advanced AI and other potential societal-scale disasters such as nuclear war, climate change and pandemics. They particularly worry about the consequences for future generations. People from the EA camp prioritize AI safety, alignment – which is the science of making sure AI systems behave in a way that is reflective of human values and in line with safety rules – and reducing the probability of catastrophic outcomes.</p>\n<p>The key difference between EAs and safetyists is that EAs generally provide funding for AI safety research and risk mitigation, whereas safetyists are researchers and advocates undertaking AI safety work and publicly calling for regulation.</p>\n<p>Other AI factions often derisively describe effective altruists as so-called \"AI Doomers.\" EA has also become a focus of criticism from the Trump administration since Coxon's resignation from Anthropic.</p>\n<p>\"Americanism, not effective altruism. The United States will continue to be AI DOMINANT!\" said one Defense Department post this month.</p>\n<p>Well-known people in the EA movement include Eliezer Yudkowsky, co-founder of the Machine Intelligence Research Institute (MIRI), which focuses on mathematical approaches to making advanced AI systems safe. He is also the author of Harry Potter fan fiction that helped popularize ideas associated with EA. Holden Karnofsky, an American entrepreneur and philanthropist, founded Coefficient Giving (formerly Open Philanthropy), an organization that funds causes central to the EA mission, including AI and biosecurity. Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei is considered to be aligned with effective altruism. Karnofsky also works at Anthropic and is married to Daniela Amodei, who is a co-founder of Anthropic and the sister of Dario Amodei, the CEO.</p>\n<p>Another high-profile supporter of the EA movement is Sam Bankman-Fried, who founded major cryptocurrency exchange FTX. In 2023, he was convicted of seven counts of fraud and conspiracy related to the misuse of customer and investor funds and is currently serving a 25-year prison sentence .</p>\n<p>The AI ethics camp argues that society should focus on the harms AI is already causing, rather than on hypothetical existential risks. Some proponents contend that emphasizing existential threats from a hypothetical future superintelligence can serve as a distraction from the more immediate harms and accountability issues surrounding AI today.</p>\n<p>AI ethicists' concerns include how AI systems can be biased due to the content of their training data, for example, the ways in which AI facial recognition or job application systems might perpetuate existing societal biases. They are also concerned with how AI could be used to exploit workers and to enable mass surveillance, as well as about the environmental costs of data centers, autonomous weapons, and the concentration of power among technology companies.</p>\n<p>A notable voice of the AI ethics faction is Timnit Gebru, a computer scientist and former co-lead of Google's Ethical AI team. Gebru left the company in 2020 because of a dispute with the company over a research paper she wrote exposing problems with large language models (LLMs), including their tendency to generate harmful or misleading language.</p>\n<p>People belonging to the \"AI as a Normal Technology\" group reject both the utopian and dystopian narratives about AI. Instead, this faction sees AI as a powerful but ultimately normal technology, comparable to the internet or electricity. They believe the focus should be on understanding and regulating specific applications and harms instead of treating AI as an unprecedented threat or as a path to inevitable superintelligence.</p>\n<p>Notable people associated with this worldview include Fei-Fei Li, a computer scientist and co-director of the Stanford Human-Centered AI Institute. Li is famous for creating ImageNet, a vastly large dataset of images which provided the training data for large neural networks, which led to several major developments in AI.</p>\n<p>Katie McQue's reporting is supported by the Tarbell Center for AI Journalism, which is a grantee of Coefficient Giving. Neither Tarbell nor Coefficient have any editorial input into her work.</p>"}}, "glossary": [{"name": "核心替换词", "words": [{"word": "faction", "level": "C1", "match": ["faction"], "note": "高/原文: the different factions ｜ 中: six groups ｜ 低: different groups"}, {"word": "whistleblower", "level": "C1", "match": ["whistleblower"], "note": "中/高: a researcher-turned-whistleblower ｜ 低: a researcher, left his job"}, {"word": "existential", "level": "C1", "match": ["existential"], "note": "高: existential risks ｜ 中: as bad as human extinction ｜ 低: the end"}, {"word": "autonomous", "level": "B2", "match": ["autonomous"], "note": "高: autonomous machines / becomes autonomous ｜ 中: acting on its own ｜ 低: work alone"}, {"word": "alignment", "level": "C1", "match": ["alignment"], "note": "主题词，三版都保留 ｜ the science of making AI follow human values"}, {"word": "surpass", "level": "C1", "match": ["surpass"], "note": "中/高: surpass human intelligence ｜ 低: become smarter than people"}, {"word": "extinction", "level": "B2", "match": ["extinction"], "note": "中/高: human extinction ｜ 低: end human life"}, {"word": "oversight", "level": "B2", "match": ["oversight"], "note": "中/高: government oversight ｜ 低: 未提及"}, {"word": "accountability", "level": "C1", "match": ["accountability"], "note": "高: company accountability ｜ 中: making companies answer for the damage"}, {"word": "treaty", "level": "C1", "match": ["treaty"], "note": "中/高: an international treaty with China ｜ 低: 未提及"}]}, {"name": "B2 档替换词", "words": [{"word": "regulation", "level": "B2", "match": ["regulation", "regulating"], "note": "中/高: calling for regulation ｜ 低: safety rules / make rules"}, {"word": "restrict", "level": "B2", "match": ["restrict"], "note": "中/高: accelerated, not restricted ｜ 低: do not slow AI down"}, {"word": "resign", "level": "B2", "match": ["resigning", "resigned"], "note": "高/原文: resigning from ｜ 中: leaving ｜ 低: left his job"}, {"word": "accuse", "level": "B2", "match": ["accusing", "accused"], "note": "中/高: accusing the companies ｜ 低: he said"}, {"word": "incident", "level": "B2", "match": ["incidents"], "note": "中/高: admitted several incidents ｜ 低: admitted something uncomfortable"}, {"word": "hack", "level": "B2", "match": ["hack"], "note": "中/高: hack Hugging Face ｜ 低: break into"}, {"word": "automation", "level": "B2", "match": ["automation"], "note": "中/高: past waves of automation ｜ 低: machines will do the work"}, {"word": "biased", "level": "B2", "match": ["biased", "biases"], "note": "高: becoming biased ｜ 中: learns unfair things ｜ 低: treats people unfairly"}, {"word": "permanent", "level": "B2", "match": ["permanent"], "note": "中/高: a permanent ban ｜ 低: ban（未提 permanent）"}, {"word": "ban", "level": "B1", "match": ["ban"], "note": "中/低: ban on building AI smarter than humans ｜ 高: 原文同"}, {"word": "dominance", "level": "C1", "match": ["dominance"], "note": "高: U.S. dominance in the global AI race ｜ 中: America must win the AI race"}, {"word": "nationalistic", "level": "C1", "match": ["nationalistic"], "note": "高: more nationalistic ｜ 中: adds politics ｜ 低: the \"America first\" group"}, {"word": "ideological", "level": "C1", "match": ["ideological"], "note": "高: an ideological divide ｜ 中/低: 未用"}, {"word": "coalition", "level": "C1", "match": ["coalition"], "note": "高: a split inside Trump's coalition ｜ 中: Trump's own side ｜ 低: some Trump supporters"}, {"word": "administration", "level": "B2", "match": ["administration"], "note": "高: the Trump administration ｜ 中: helps Trump ｜ 低: 未提及"}, {"word": "adviser", "level": "B2", "match": ["adviser"], "note": "高: White House AI adviser ｜ 中: who helps Trump on AI questions"}, {"word": "candidate", "level": "B1", "match": ["candidates"], "note": "高: support pro-AI candidates ｜ 中/低: 略去该细节"}]}, {"name": "仅高版出现", "words": [{"word": "catastrophic", "level": "C1", "match": ["catastrophic"], "note": "高: catastrophic or existential results ｜ 中: as bad as human extinction ｜ 低: the end"}, {"word": "probability", "level": "B2", "match": ["probability"], "note": "高: lowering the probability of catastrophic outcomes ｜ 中/低: 简化掉"}, {"word": "advocate", "level": "C1", "match": ["advocates"], "note": "高: researchers and advocates ｜ 中: do the work and ask for regulation"}, {"word": "clash", "level": "C1", "match": ["clash"], "note": "高: a simple clash between two sides ｜ 中: two sides fighting ｜ 低: the newest fight"}, {"word": "governance", "level": "C1", "match": ["governance"], "note": "高: views of ... governance ｜ 中/低: 简化掉"}, {"word": "utopian / dystopian", "level": "C1", "match": ["utopian", "dystopian"], "note": "高: the utopian and dystopian stories ｜ 中: the dream or the dark story ｜ 低: don't panic"}, {"word": "swarm", "level": "C1", "match": ["swarms"], "note": "高: swarms of agents ｜ 中: large groups of agents ｜ 低: 未用"}, {"word": "exploit", "level": "B2", "match": ["exploiting"], "note": "高: exploiting workers ｜ 中: hard pressure on workers ｜ 低: lost jobs"}, {"word": "dataset", "level": "C1", "match": ["dataset"], "note": "高: the huge picture dataset（ImageNet）｜ 中/低: 未提 Fei-Fei Li 段"}, {"word": "mass surveillance", "level": "C1", "match": ["mass surveillance"], "note": "高: mass surveillance ｜ 中: watching everyone ｜ 低: too much watching of ordinary people"}]}, {"name": "引语保留与原文难词", "words": [{"word": "superintelligence", "level": "超罕见", "match": ["superintelligence", "superintelligent"], "note": "引语原词 + 高版术语 ｜ 中: AI smarter than humans ｜ 低: smarter than people"}, {"word": "gambling", "level": "C1", "match": ["gambling"], "note": "引语 \"gambling with our lives\" ｜ 低转述: playing with our lives"}, {"word": "quarantine", "level": "B2", "match": ["quarantine"], "note": "高保留 Bannon 引语原话 ｜ We should quarantine now every aspect..."}, {"word": "aspect", "level": "B2", "match": ["aspect"], "note": "同上引语 ｜ every aspect of the ecosystem"}, {"word": "ecosystem", "level": "B2", "match": ["ecosystem"], "note": "同上引语 ｜ the ecosystem of artificial intelligence"}, {"word": "crescendo", "level": "C2", "match": ["crescendo"], "note": "原文 reached a crescendo ｜ 高: a boiling point ｜ 中: become loud"}, {"word": "supercharged", "level": "C2", "match": ["supercharged"], "note": "原文 claims supercharged the debate ｜ 高: gave huge energy ｜ 低: After that"}, {"word": "wreak", "level": "C2", "match": ["wreak"], "note": "原文 damage AI could wreak ｜ 高/中: the damage AI could cause ｜ 低: 未提"}, {"word": "stint", "level": "B2", "match": ["stint"], "note": "原文 after a previous stint ｜ 高: after a period ｜ 中: after a time"}, {"word": "propel", "level": "C1", "match": ["propel"], "note": "原文 propel medical advancements ｜ 高: push medicine forward ｜ 中/低: better medicine"}, {"word": "unencumbered", "level": "C2", "match": ["unencumbered"], "note": "原文 unencumbered by regulation ｜ 高: free from regulation ｜ 低: do not slow AI down"}, {"word": "perpetuate", "level": "C1", "match": ["perpetuate"], "note": "原文 perpetuate existing societal biases ｜ 高: repeat unfair treatment ｜ 低: treats people unfairly"}, {"word": "tendency", "level": "B2", "match": ["tendency"], "note": "原文 their tendency to generate ｜ 高: their habit of ｜ 低: 未提"}, {"word": "misleading", "level": "C1", "match": ["misleading"], "note": "原文 harmful or misleading language ｜ 高: made-up answers ｜ 低: 未提"}]}], "nWords": 4155}, {"slug": "npr-south-africa", "title": "South Africa 'feels like a prison' for women", "source": "NPR", "sourceUrl": "https://text.npr.org/nx-s1-5973822", "author": "Kate Bartlett", "date": "2026-09-18", "added": "2026-09-27", "tags": ["news", "society"], "versions": {"low": {"label": "低", "density": 1.2, "target": "<2%", "desc": "A2 词汇摘编，短句主线", "html": "<p>Elizabeth Moselakgomo was a 38-year-old manager and a mother. She loved running near her home in Johannesburg, South Africa's biggest city.</p>\n<p>Last week she went out for her usual afternoon run — and never came home. Her 8-year-old daughter called family, and the family called the police. Five days later, police found her body. Someone had hit her face very hard.</p>\n<p>Police say nine women were killed in the same area in a few weeks. Many of them were sexually attacked too.</p>\n<p>South Africa already has one of the highest rates of men killing and hurting women in the world. Police statistics say about 15 women are killed every day.</p>\n<p>People are surprised, angry and afraid. Police told women: do not walk or run alone. Many women are angry at this. They say: the police must protect us — we should not have to change our lives.</p>\n<p>President Cyril Ramaphosa says the police will work hard to find the killer or killers.</p>\n<p>Women marched in the streets of Johannesburg and Cape Town to protest. There were also runs in many cities to remember Elizabeth and the other women. Men and women wore black and stood quiet for one minute before they ran.</p>\n<p>One runner, 26-year-old Shari Maluluke, told NPR: \"You go for a run — you can be killed. You go to the post office — you can be killed. Even in your own home — you can be killed. I cannot go where I want. My whole country feels like a prison.\"</p>"}, "mid": {"label": "中", "density": 4.5, "target": "2–5%", "desc": "i+1 理想区", "html": "<p>Elizabeth Moselakgomo, a 38-year-old manager and mother, loved running in her neighborhood near the Johannesburg airport. Last week she went out for her usual afternoon run — and never returned.</p>\n<p>Her 8-year-old daughter called relatives, and they called the police. Five days later, police found her body. Her face had been hit so hard that nobody could recognise her.</p>\n<p>Police say she was one of nine women found killed in the same area of Johannesburg in a few weeks. The majority of the women were sexually assaulted.</p>\n<p>The killings have shocked South Africans — although the country already has some of the highest rates of violence against women in the world. Police statistics say more than 100 rape cases are reported every day, and about 15 women are killed daily.</p>\n<p>Police have not ruled out the possibility that one killer is responsible. They have warned women to be very careful and not to walk or run alone. Many women are angry at this advice. They say protecting women is the police's job — women should not have to change how they live.</p>\n<p>President Cyril Ramaphosa has promised that the police will do everything to find the killer or killers. But many people say the government has not done enough. In 2025, the government declared violence against women a national disaster.</p>\n<p>\"Women in South Africa continue to be hunted and denied the most basic right to life and security,\" said Shenilla Mohamed of Amnesty International. \"Women are tired of empty words. We want to know what will be done to make women and children feel safe.\"</p>\n<p>Women marched in the streets in Kempton Park, the suburb where many of the killings happened, and also in Cape Town. \"Enough is enough,\" they said.</p>\n<p>Elizabeth loved running, so running clubs across the country are holding runs in her memory and in memory of all the victims. At one run in Johannesburg, people of all ages wore black and stood in silence for a minute before they started.</p>\n<p>\"You go for a run, you can be killed. You go to the post office, you can be killed. Even in your own home, you can be killed,\" said Shari Maluluke, 26. \"It makes me angry that I cannot move freely. My whole country feels like a prison.\"</p>\n<p>All the women NPR spoke to said they did not feel safe. Didi Tsitsi, 31, described the precautions women take: \"You send your location, you share your Ubers.\"</p>\n<p>Why is the killing of women such a big problem in South Africa? \"The government and the police have failed,\" Tsitsi said. \"There is also a deep problem in society about what men are brought up to be.\" And, she added, South Africa has never really dealt with the legacy of apartheid.</p>"}, "high": {"label": "高", "density": 7.7, "target": "5–8%", "desc": "接近原文，引语原样保留", "html": "<p>Elizabeth Moselakgomo, a 38-year-old manager, mother and keen runner, went out for her usual afternoon run in her neighborhood near the Johannesburg airport last week — and never returned.</p>\n<p>When she failed to come home, her 8-year-old daughter called relatives, who contacted the police. Five days later, Moselakgomo's body was found in the area, half-naked, her face beaten so badly that nobody could tell who she was.</p>\n<p>Moselakgomo is one of nine women who police say have been found dead, most of them within a few weeks, in the same area of South Africa's biggest city. Most were sexually assaulted.</p>\n<p>Even in a country notorious for some of the highest rates of gender-based violence in the world — with over 100 rape cases reported and some 15 women killed daily, according to police statistics — the latest spate of murders has left South Africans shocked, angry and afraid.</p>\n<p>The police have not ruled out a serial killer and have warned women to be \"vigilant\" and not to walk or run alone. The approach has drawn backlash from women on social media, who say the onus should be on the police to protect them — not on them to limit their own movements.</p>\n<p>President Cyril Ramaphosa has promised that \"no stone will be left unturned\" in the hunt for the perpetrator or perpetrators. But many South Africans say the government — which declared gender-based violence and femicide a national disaster in 2025 — has not done enough to keep women and girls safe.</p>\n<p>\"Women in South Africa continue to be hunted and denied the most basic right to life and security,\" said Amnesty International's South Africa director Shenilla Mohamed. \"Women are tired of platitudes. We want to know what is going to be done to make women and children feel safe.\"</p>\n<p>There have been protests in Kempton Park, the suburb where many of the killings have happened, as well as in Cape Town, with South African women saying: enough is enough.</p>\n<p>Because Moselakgomo was a keen runner, local running clubs across the country have been holding runs in her honour and in remembrance of all the victims. At one in Johannesburg, men and women of all ages and races came dressed in black and held a minute's silence before setting off.</p>\n<p>\"Gender-based violence, but particularly femicide, is such a big issue in South Africa. As a woman you can't do anything alone,\" one of the runners, 26-year-old Shari Maluluke, told NPR.</p>\n<p>\"You jog, you can get killed. You go to the post office, you can get killed. In your homes you can get killed… It just makes me angry that I can't move freely — my whole country feels like a prison.\"</p>\n<p>All the women NPR spoke to at the event said they did not feel safe in South Africa. \"You send your location, you share your Ubers,\" said 31-year-old Didi Tsitsi, describing the precautions women have to take.</p>\n<p>Asked why she thought the killing of women was such a big problem in South Africa, Tsitsi said there were a number of factors: the failure of government and policing, and \"a deep-rooted societal issue of what men are brought up to be and what they believe it is to be a man\".</p>\n<p>And, she added, South Africa still has not really dealt with all the legacies of apartheid — this, she thinks, is one of the ways it is showing itself.</p>\n<p>Some academic research supports this idea. Studies suggest that the years of brutal white-minority rule, under which Black men lost agency and autonomy, have led to a crisis of masculinity and the entrenchment of patriarchal attitudes.</p>"}, "original": {"label": "原文", "density": 9.2, "target": "—", "desc": "NPR 原文", "html": "<p>JOHANNESBURG — Elizabeth Moselakgomo, a 38-year-old manager, mother, and keen runner, went out for her usual afternoon jog in her neighborhood near the Johannesburg airport last week and never returned.</p>\n<p>When she failed to come home, her 8-year-old daughter called relatives, who contacted the police. Five days later Moselakgomo's half-naked body was found in the area, with her face so badly beaten it was unrecognisable.</p>\n<p>Moselakgomo is one of nine women who police say have been found slain, most of them in a period of a few weeks, in the same area of South Africa's biggest city. The majority of them were sexually assaulted.</p>\n<p>Even in a country notorious for some of the highest rates of gender-based violence in the world – with over 100 rape cases reported and some 15 women killed daily, according to police statistics -- the latest spate of murders has left South Africans shocked, angry and afraid.</p>\n<p>Police haven't ruled out a serial killer and have warned women to be \"vigilant\" and not to walk or run alone. The approach is drawing backlash from women on social media who said the onus should be on the police to protect them, not for them to have to curtail their movements.</p>\n<p>President Cyril Ramaphosa has promised \"no stone will be left unturned\" in the hunt for the perpetrator or perpetrators. But many South Africans say the government – which declared gender-based violence and femicide a national disaster in 2025 -- has not done enough to keep women and girls safe.</p>\n<p>\"Women in South Africa continue to be hunted and denied the most basic right to life and security,\" said Amnesty International's South Africa Director Shenilla Mohamed. \"Women are tired of platitudes. We want to know what is going to be done to make women and children feel safe.\"</p>\n<p>There have been protests in Kempton Park, the suburb where many of the killings have been, as well as in Cape Town, with South African women saying enough is enough.</p>\n<p>As Moselakgomo was a keen runner, many local running clubs across the country have been holding runs in her honour and in remembrance of all the victims. At one in Johannesburg, men and women of all ages and races came dressed in black and held a minute's silence before they set off.</p>\n<p>\"Gender-based violence, but particularly femicide, is such a big issue in South Africa. As a woman you can't do anything alone,\" one of the runners, 26-year-old Shari Maluluke, told NPR.</p>\n<p>\"You jog, you can get killed, you go to the post office, you can get killed, in your homes you can get killed…It just makes me angry that I can't move freely, my whole country feels like a prison.\"</p>\n<p>All the women NPR spoke to at the event said they did not feel safe in South Africa.</p>\n<p>\"You send your location, you share your Ubers,\" said 31-year-old Didi Tsitsi of the precautions women have to take.</p>\n<p>Asked why she thought femicide was such a big problem in South Africa, Tsitsi said she thought there were a number of factors.</p>\n<p>\"Failure of government and policing, I do think there's a deep-rooted societal issue of what men are brought up to be and what they believe it is to be a man,\" she said.</p>\n<p>\"And we still haven't really, I think, dealt with all of the legacies of apartheid – this is I think one of the ways it's manifesting.\"</p>\n<p>Some academic research supports this idea, with studies suggesting years of brutal white-minority rule, under which Black men were denied agency and autonomy, has led to a crisis of masculinity and the entrenchment of patriarchal attitudes.</p>"}}, "glossary": [{"name": "核心替换词", "words": [{"word": "perpetrator", "level": "C1", "match": ["perpetrator"], "note": "中/低: the killer or killers ｜ 高: the perpetrator or perpetrators"}, {"word": "vigilant", "level": "C1", "match": ["vigilant"], "note": "中: be very careful ｜ 高: 警方原话 \"be vigilant\""}, {"word": "notorious", "level": "C1", "match": ["notorious"], "note": "高: a country notorious for... ｜ 中: 转述为 highest rates of violence against women"}, {"word": "assault", "level": "C1", "match": ["assault"], "note": "中/高: sexually assaulted ｜ 低: sexually attacked"}, {"word": "rape", "level": "C1", "match": ["rape"], "note": "中/高: rape cases ｜ 低: 略去该数据"}, {"word": "precaution", "level": "C1", "match": ["precaution"], "note": "中/高: the precautions women take ｜ 低: 未提及"}, {"word": "legacy", "level": "C1", "match": ["legacy"], "note": "中/高: the legacy of apartheid ｜ 低: 未提该段"}, {"word": "spate", "level": "C2", "match": ["spate"], "note": "高: the latest spate of murders ｜ 中: a series of killings"}, {"word": "onus", "level": "C2", "match": ["onus"], "note": "高: the onus should be on the police ｜ 中: the police's job"}, {"word": "curtail", "level": "C2", "match": ["curtail"], "note": "高版原文 curtail their movements，已简化为 limit"}, {"word": "unturned", "level": "C2", "match": ["unturned"], "note": "高: \"no stone will be left unturned\" ｜ 中: will do everything"}, {"word": "platitude", "level": "超罕见", "match": ["platitude"], "note": "高: tired of platitudes ｜ 中: empty words"}, {"word": "femicide", "level": "超罕见", "match": ["femicide"], "note": "主题词 ｜ 高: 保留原词 ｜ 中: the killing of women"}, {"word": "serial", "level": "C1", "match": ["serial"], "note": "高: a serial killer ｜ 中: one killer is responsible"}, {"word": "slain", "level": "C1", "match": ["slain"], "note": "高版原文 found slain，已简化为 found dead"}]}, {"name": "B2 档替换词", "words": [{"word": "gender-based violence", "level": "B2", "match": ["gender-based violence"], "note": "高: 保留 ×3 ｜ 中: violence against women ｜ 低: men killing and hurting women"}, {"word": "majority", "level": "B2", "match": ["majority"], "note": "中: The majority were... ｜ 高: 已简化为 most ｜ 低: many of them"}, {"word": "deny", "level": "B2", "match": ["deny"], "note": "中/高(Amnesty引语): denied the most basic right ｜ 高(研究段): 已简化为 lost"}, {"word": "shocked", "level": "B2", "match": ["shocked"], "note": "中/高: shocked ｜ 低: surprised"}, {"word": "silence", "level": "B2", "match": ["silence"], "note": "中/高: stood in silence ｜ 低: stood quiet"}, {"word": "freely", "level": "B2", "match": ["freely"], "note": "中/高: move freely ｜ 低: go where I want"}, {"word": "suburb", "level": "B2", "match": ["suburb"], "note": "中/高: the suburb ｜ 低: the same area"}, {"word": "failure", "level": "B2", "match": ["failure"], "note": "高: the failure of government and policing ｜ 中: the police have failed"}, {"word": "backlash", "level": "B2", "match": ["backlash"], "note": "高: drawn backlash ｜ 中: many women are angry at this"}, {"word": "recognise", "level": "B2", "match": ["recognise"], "note": "中: nobody could recognise her ｜ 高: 已简化为 tell who she was"}, {"word": "honour / remembrance", "level": "B2", "match": ["honour", "remembrance"], "note": "高: in her honour / in remembrance of ｜ 中: in her memory / in memory of"}, {"word": "naked", "level": "B2", "match": ["naked"], "note": "高: half-naked ｜ 中/低: 略去细节"}]}, {"name": "仅高版出现（学术分析段）", "words": [{"word": "societal", "level": "C1", "match": ["societal"], "note": "中: a deep problem in society"}, {"word": "deep-rooted", "level": "C1", "match": ["deep-rooted"], "note": "高: a deep-rooted societal issue ｜ 中: a deep problem in society"}, {"word": "masculinity", "level": "C1", "match": ["masculinity"], "note": "高版研究段：男性气质危机"}, {"word": "patriarchal", "level": "C1", "match": ["patriarchal"], "note": "高版研究段：父权观念"}, {"word": "autonomy", "level": "C1", "match": ["autonomy"], "note": "高版研究段：自主权"}, {"word": "brutal", "level": "C1", "match": ["brutal"], "note": "高: brutal white-minority rule"}, {"word": "entrenchment", "level": "C2", "match": ["entrenchment"], "note": "高版研究段：固化、根深蒂固"}, {"word": "agency", "level": "B2", "match": ["agency"], "note": "高版研究段：行动能力、自主"}, {"word": "minority", "level": "B2", "match": ["minority"], "note": "高: white-minority rule"}, {"word": "crisis", "level": "B2", "match": ["crisis"], "note": "高版研究段：a crisis of masculinity"}]}, {"name": "词表内搭配（非生词，供注意）", "words": [{"word": "keen runner", "level": "B1", "match": ["keen"], "note": "高: a keen runner ｜ 中: loved running（keen = 热衷的）"}, {"word": "jog", "level": "C1", "match": ["jog"], "note": "高: You jog, you can get killed（引语保留）｜ 中/低: run"}]}], "nWords": 1966}]};
